    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d294">

                <pb facs="0480" n="166" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg166"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/317</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Guthrie</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-08-11T13:00:00+09:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 11, 1914, 1 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8 a.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Counselor of German Embassy informs me that his Ambassador anticipates
                    proposition from Japan for neutralization of Orient. Ambassador has no direct
                    communication with Berlin and he requests you to inform his Government and says
                    that he advises this arrangement and desires instructions so that he may be
                    prepared to act if proposition is made. Unless the arrangement is made
                    Ambassador believes that attack will be made on German possessions and considers
                    resistance hopeless. I have had no communications with the Foreign Office on the
                    subject.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Guthrie</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
